   "text": {
    "L1": "The poem is about focus: doing one thing at a time and doing it well.",
    "L2": "It compares one careful thing with ten things done here and there.",
    "L3": "The message is clear: one thing done with care is better.",
    "L4": "For example, do your homework without TV, so you can focus and finish it well.",
    "L5": "The poem also has rhyme. Care and there rhyme even though they are spelt differently.",
    "L6": "When we describe a habit with he or she, add -s to the action word: She reads every day.",
    "L7": "Here is a grammar myth: “She play every day.” It is wrong because he or she needs -s: “She plays every day.”",
    "L8": "Now choose the habit sentence that follows the poem’s careful language rule."
   },
